MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two pieces:

1. transports/bifrost-http/handlers/mt_integration_test.go — Layer 1
   E2E. Boots a real SQLite ConfigStore via the production
   NewConfigStore constructor (real migration chain, real default
   tenant seeding), wires the platform-admin tenants handler, the
   tenant-scoped provider handler, and the tenant-scoped VK handler
   into a fasthttp router, then drives the full happy path:

   - POST /api/platform/tenants for acme + globex.
   - POST /api/tenants/{tid}/providers with distinct concurrency
     settings so cross-tenant leakage would be observable.
   - POST /api/tenants/{tid}/governance/virtual-keys per tenant.

   Then asserts the three layers that matter for tenant isolation:

   - configstore.GetProvidersConfigByTenant returns disjoint configs
     with the expected concurrency per tenant.
   - multitenant.NewConfigStoreVKResolver maps each minted VK back
     to its tenant id.
   - multitenant.Manager.Acquire lazy-loads a per-tenant runtime via
     a loader that reads providers through GetProvidersConfigByTenant,
     and the loader is invoked once per tenant.

   A second test confirms RequireTenantPathMiddleware rejects
   cross-tenant writes (tenant-admin trying to act on another tenant
   -> 403) so Phase 4's real AuthzPlugin can swap in without
   regressing the policy.

   Runs in <1s, no Docker dependency — gates every MR.

2. test/e2e/README.md — Layer 2 scaffold. Documents the kind/Helm/
   Postgres E2E suite that lives alongside Layer 1: proposed layout,
   what Layer 2 covers that Layer 1 cannot (network failures,
   multi-replica coordination, Helm/k8s drift, real-provider rate
   limits), and the open questions that gate building it (existing
   Helm chart status, mock-provider choice, CI cadence).

Ref: https://jira.f5net.com/browse/XC-25496
